## Elevation models

Terrain is the covariate behind almost everything else in a forest. It drives
productivity, access, erosion, and where water collects. It arrives as a digital
elevation model (DEM), a raster whose cell value is height above a datum.

This chapter works on Lake Chilwa in southern Malawi, the area of interest used
throughout the companion map-templates material. Chilwa is a shallow endorheic
lake: water enters from the surrounding hills and leaves only by evaporation.
There is no outlet. Its area swings by a factor of several between wet and dry
years, and it has dried out completely three times in living memory, in 1968,
1995 and 2012. That makes it a hard case for watershed delineation, whose
standard algorithms assume drainage eventually reaches the sea, and an obvious
case for inundation mapping, which is the deliverable a wetland project needs.

In operational work you download elevation for your own site. The `elevatr`
package fetches open tiles for any area:

```{r}
#| eval: false
library(elevatr)
dem <- get_elev_raster(aoi, z = 10, clip = "locations") |> rast()
```

So the book builds offline, `data-raw/prepare-chilwa-data.R` did this once and
committed the result. The grid is EPSG:3857 at 100 m, matching the
map-templates workflow, and covers the lake plus 60 km of margin so the
catchment divide falls inside the raster rather than being clipped by it:

```{r}
library(terra)
library(sf)
library(whitebox)

sf_use_s2(FALSE)

dem    <- rast("data/chilwa_dem.tif")
lake   <- st_read("data/chilwa_hydro.gpkg", layer = "lake",   quiet = TRUE)
basin  <- st_read("data/chilwa_hydro.gpkg", layer = "basin",  quiet = TRUE)
rivers <- st_read("data/chilwa_hydro.gpkg", layer = "rivers", quiet = TRUE)

dim(dem)
global(dem, range, na.rm = TRUE)
```

### Projection distortion

One property of the grid has to be settled before any area is quoted, because
it silently corrupts every hydrological number that follows.

EPSG:3857 is a Mercator projection. It is conformal, not equal-area, and its
scale factor grows with latitude. A cell that is nominally 100 m square does not
cover a hectare of ground anywhere except the equator. At Chilwa's latitude the
error is large enough to matter:

```{r}
cell_ha <- cellSize(dem, unit = "ha")
global(cell_ha, range, na.rm = TRUE)
```

The convenient identity that one 100 m cell equals one hectare, so a flow
accumulation count multiplied by 0.01 gives square kilometres, is therefore
wrong here by about seven per cent. It is a tempting shortcut and it appears in
a great deal of published hydrological code. This chapter uses `cellSize()`
throughout and reports areas after reprojection to UTM zone 36S (EPSG:32736),
which is equal-area enough at this scale to serve as truth.

## Terrain derivatives

Elevation is rarely the variable of interest. What it implies, slope, aspect and
ruggedness, is. `terrain()` derives these directly. Slope and aspect need a
projected DEM so that horizontal and vertical distances share units:

```{r}
slope  <- terrain(dem, "slope",  unit = "degrees")
aspect <- terrain(dem, "aspect", unit = "degrees")
tri    <- terrain(dem, "TRI")

global(slope, c("mean", "max"), na.rm = TRUE)
```

Slope governs operability and erosion risk. Ruggedness is a useful productivity
covariate, because sheltered, gentler terrain often carries taller and denser
stands. A hillshade turns terrain into an intuitive relief image by simulating
illumination, and is the standard base layer under any map:

```{r}
#| fig-cap: "Relief of the Lake Chilwa basin. The lake occupies the flat floor at centre; the Zomba and Mulanje massifs rise on the western and southern margins."
hs <- shade(terrain(dem, "slope",  unit = "radians"),
            terrain(dem, "aspect", unit = "radians"),
            angle = 40, direction = 315)

plot(hs, col = grey(0:100 / 100), legend = FALSE, mar = c(2, 2, 2, 2))
plot(st_geometry(lake),  add = TRUE, border = "steelblue", lwd = 2)
plot(st_geometry(basin), add = TRUE, border = "orangered", lwd = 1.5)
```

## Hydraulic conditioning

Water moves downhill, so a DEM already encodes drainage. It does not encode it
cleanly. Every DEM contains spurious depressions: cells with no lower neighbour,
produced by interpolation error, vegetation returns, and the finite vertical
precision of the sensor. A routing algorithm entering one of these has nowhere
to send the water, and the drainage network fragments into disconnected pieces.

Conditioning removes the artefacts while preserving the real depressions. For
Chilwa that distinction is the whole problem, because the lake is itself a
depression, and one the algorithm must not fill:

```{r}
dir.create("data/chilwa-hydro", showWarnings = FALSE)
wd <- "data/chilwa-hydro/"
file.copy("data/chilwa_dem.tif", paste0(wd, "dem.tif"), overwrite = TRUE)

wbt_depth_in_sink(dem = "dem.tif", output = "sink_depth.tif",
                  wd = wd, zero_background = TRUE, verbose_mode = FALSE)

sink_depth <- rast(paste0(wd, "sink_depth.tif"))
global(sink_depth > 0, "sum", na.rm = TRUE)[1, 1]
global(sink_depth, "max", na.rm = TRUE)[1, 1]
```

Roughly half a million cells sit in some depression, and the deepest sits
hundreds of metres below its spillover point. No threshold on depth alone
separates real basins from interpolation pits, because the two overlap in size:
the deepest single pit in this grid is an artefact, not the lake.

### Stochastic depressions

Depth cannot make the distinction, but uncertainty can. A DEM cell is an
estimate with an error, and a depression that survives being perturbed by that
error is probably real, while one that disappears was probably never there.

Lindsay's stochastic depression analysis [@lindsay2006distinguishing] formalises
this as a Monte Carlo experiment. Each iteration adds a Gaussian error field to
the DEM, drawn with the sensor's root mean square error and a spatial
autocorrelation range, then identifies the depressions in the perturbed surface.
The output is the proportion of iterations in which each cell was in a
depression, which reads directly as a probability. Radar DEMs at this
resolution carry a vertical error of around three metres, which sets `rmse`:

```{r}
wbt_stochastic_depression_analysis(dem = "dem.tif", output = "sink_prob.tif",
                                   wd = wd, rmse = 3.0, range = 10.0,
                                   iterations = 50, verbose_mode = FALSE)

sink_prob <- rast(paste0(wd, "sink_prob.tif"))

data.frame(
  threshold = c(0.2, 0.5, 0.8),
  cells     = vapply(c(0.2, 0.5, 0.8),
                     function(p) global(sink_prob >= p, "sum", na.rm = TRUE)[1, 1],
                     numeric(1))
)
```

Raising the threshold from 0.2 to 0.8 discards about three quarters of the
candidate depressions as artefacts of DEM error. The question is which side of
that line the lake falls on:

```{r}
lake_mask <- rasterize(vect(lake), dem)
round(global(ifel(!is.na(lake_mask), sink_prob, NA), "mean", na.rm = TRUE)[1, 1], 3)
```

Effectively unity. The lake is a depression in every iteration, which is the
evidence needed to treat it as a genuine terminal sink rather than something to
be conditioned away. Running this before conditioning, rather than assuming a
depth threshold will separate signal from noise, is the difference between a
defensible endorheic analysis and a lucky one.

### Depression breaching

Two families of algorithm exist. Filling raises the floor of a depression until
it overflows, which is simple but destroys the depression. Breaching carves a
channel out through the lowest point of the rim, which changes far fewer cells
and preserves the basin's shape.

`WhiteboxTools` implements Lindsay's hybrid method [@lindsay2016efficient],
which breaches what it can within a depth limit and fills whatever remains. The
two parameters do the work. `max_depth` caps how deep a channel may be carved,
so shallow artefacts are breached while a genuine 400 m basin is left alone.
`flat_increment` adds a small monotonic gradient along the carved channel, so
flow directions stay unambiguous across ground the DEM records as perfectly
level:

```{r}
t_breach <- system.time(
  wbt_breach_depressions(dem = "dem.tif", output = "breached.tif", wd = wd,
                         max_depth = 3, flat_increment = 0.001,
                         verbose_mode = FALSE)
)

breached <- rast(paste0(wd, "breached.tif"))
carved   <- dem - breached

data.frame(
  cells_changed = global(carved != 0, "sum", na.rm = TRUE)[1, 1],
  max_carve_m   = round(global(carved, "max", na.rm = TRUE)[1, 1], 2),
  max_raise_m   = round(-global(carved, "min", na.rm = TRUE)[1, 1], 2),
  seconds       = round(t_breach[["elapsed"]], 1)
)
```

Read both columns. The carving obeys its limit exactly: no cell is lowered more
than the three metres imposed. The raising does not, because it is not
constrained by `max_depth` at all. Lindsay's method is a hybrid. It breaches
what it can within the depth limit and fills everything else, and the filling is
unbounded.

That asymmetry is easy to miss, and it is the single most consequential
behaviour in this chapter.

### Depression filling

A second pass with a filling algorithm normally follows, to clear whatever the
breaching step could not reach. Wang and Liu's scan-line method
[@wang2006efficient] is the usual choice for low-relief terrain:

```{r}
wbt_fill_depressions_wang_and_liu(dem = "breached.tif", output = "filled.tif",
                                  wd = wd, verbose_mode = FALSE)

filled <- rast(paste0(wd, "filled.tif"))
global((filled - breached) != 0, "sum", na.rm = TRUE)[1, 1]
```

Zero cells change. That is the correct result and it is worth pausing on,
because it is easy to run a fill step, see a new file appear, and assume it did
something. The breaching step already filled every depression it declined to
breach, so its output was depression-free before Wang and Liu ever saw it. The
second pass is redundant here. It is retained because it costs seconds and
because it is not redundant after a pure breaching algorithm, but the honest
report is that it changed nothing.

### Endorheic cost

If the conditioned surface has no depressions left, then it has no Lake Chilwa.
The stochastic analysis established that the lake is a real basin with
probability one, and the conditioning step then filled it in:

```{r}
raised <- breached - dem
lake_raise <- ifel(!is.na(lake_mask), raised, NA)

data.frame(
  mean_raise_m  = round(global(lake_raise, "mean", na.rm = TRUE)[1, 1], 1),
  max_raise_m   = round(global(lake_raise, "max",  na.rm = TRUE)[1, 1], 1),
  raw_surface_m = as.numeric(global(ifel(!is.na(lake_mask), dem, NA),
                                    quantile, probs = 0.5, na.rm = TRUE)),
  cond_surface_m = round(as.numeric(global(ifel(!is.na(lake_mask), breached, NA),
                                           quantile, probs = 0.5, na.rm = TRUE)), 1)
)
```

The lake bed has been raised by fourteen metres on average and by more than
eighty at its deepest, and its median surface has moved from 623 m to 637 m. In
the conditioned DEM, Chilwa is a gentle ramp that water crosses on its way
somewhere else.

This is not a bug and it is not avoidable by tuning `max_depth`. Flow routing
requires that every cell have a downhill neighbour, and a terminal sink by
definition does not. Any algorithm that produces a routable surface must remove
the sink. The published claim that a depth constraint "preserves deeper basin
structures while breaching shallow artefacts" does not survive measurement: the
constraint bounds the carving, not the filling.

Two rules follow, and they govern the rest of the chapter.

Flow routing uses the conditioned DEM, and the endorheic basin is expected to
be gone from it. That is fine, because what is wanted from routing is the
convergence pattern, and filling a flat sink with a monotonic gradient preserves
where water arrives even though it invents where it leaves.

Anything that depends on the shape of the depression, which means depression
depth, inundation extent and any stage-area relationship, uses the raw DEM. Both
`wbt_depth_in_sink` and `wbt_stochastic_depression_analysis` above were run on
`dem.tif` for exactly this reason. Running an inundation model on a conditioned
surface is a silent and total error: the basin whose flooding is being modelled
has already been filled.

## Flow routing

The D8 algorithm [@o1984extraction] sends all water from a cell to whichever of
its eight neighbours lies steepest downhill. Applied across the grid it yields a
pointer raster, and following the pointers upstream from each cell counts how
many cells drain through it. That count is flow accumulation, and it is the
basis of every stream network and catchment boundary:

```{r}
t_flow <- system.time({
  wbt_d8_pointer(dem = "filled.tif", output = "d8.tif",
                 wd = wd, verbose_mode = FALSE)
  wbt_d8_flow_accumulation(input = "d8.tif", output = "acc.tif",
                           wd = wd, pntr = TRUE, verbose_mode = FALSE)
})

acc <- rast(paste0(wd, "acc.tif"))
round(t_flow[["elapsed"]], 1)
global(acc, "max", na.rm = TRUE)[1, 1]
```

### Reading accumulation

The maximum accumulation on the grid is a little over 1.15 million cells. It is
tempting to read that as the size of the Chilwa catchment, and to convert it to
area with the one-cell-one-hectare shortcut. Both steps would be wrong.

The conversion is wrong for the reason established above: these cells are not
hectares. The interpretation is wrong for a subtler reason. The DEM extends 60
km beyond the lake in every direction, so it contains parts of neighbouring
catchments whose water leaves the grid edge. The global maximum belongs to
whichever drainage is largest, not necessarily to Chilwa. Checking where it
falls settles it:

```{r}
peak_cell <- which.max(values(acc))
peak_xy   <- xyFromCell(acc, peak_cell)

st_intersects(st_as_sf(data.frame(x = peak_xy[1], y = peak_xy[2]),
                       coords = c("x", "y"), crs = 3857),
              lake, sparse = FALSE)[1, 1]
```

The cell of maximum accumulation is not in the lake. The number describes some
other river leaving the raster, and quoting it as Chilwa's contributing area
would overstate the catchment by more than a third. The figure that matters is
the largest accumulation reached inside the lake, which is where Chilwa's own
drainage converges:

```{r}
lake_acc <- ifel(!is.na(lake_mask), acc, NA)

outlet_acc  <- global(lake_acc, "max", na.rm = TRUE)[1, 1]
outlet_cell <- which.max(values(lake_acc))
outlet_xy   <- xyFromCell(lake_acc, outlet_cell)

outlet_acc
```

## Watershed delineation

That cell is the terminal pour point: the single location on which the whole
endorheic system converges. Delineation traces the pointer raster upstream from
it and returns every cell that drains there:

```{r}
outlet <- st_as_sf(data.frame(x = outlet_xy[1], y = outlet_xy[2]),
                   coords = c("x", "y"), crs = 3857)
st_write(outlet, paste0(wd, "outlet.shp"), delete_dsn = TRUE, quiet = TRUE)

wbt_watershed(d8_pntr = "d8.tif", pour_pts = "outlet.shp",
              output = "watershed.tif", wd = wd, verbose_mode = FALSE)

ws <- rast(paste0(wd, "watershed.tif"))
ws_poly <- as.polygons(ws) |> st_as_sf() |> st_transform(32736)
ws_km2  <- sum(as.numeric(st_area(ws_poly))) / 1e6

round(ws_km2, 1)
```

### Independent comparison

A delineated catchment is a claim, and claims get checked. HydroSHEDS publishes
an independent global product, HydroBASINS, derived from a different DEM by a
different group. Comparing against it is the cheapest validation available:

```{r}
basin_utm <- st_transform(basin, 32736)
basin_km2 <- as.numeric(st_area(basin_utm)) / 1e6

overlap_km2 <- st_intersection(st_union(ws_poly), st_union(basin_utm)) |>
  st_area() |> as.numeric() |> sum() / 1e6

data.frame(
  source   = c("This delineation", "HydroBASINS v1.0"),
  area_km2 = round(c(ws_km2, basin_km2), 1),
  agreement_pct = round(100 * overlap_km2 / c(ws_km2, basin_km2), 1)
)
```

The two agree closely. Where they differ is along the flat eastern margin, which
is the expected place: on ground with almost no gradient, small differences in
the source DEM move the divide a long way, and neither answer is more correct
than the other. A verifier reading a catchment boundary should ask what the
relief is along the divide before treating the line as precise.

```{r}
#| fig-cap: "Delineated catchment against the HydroBASINS boundary, over the flow accumulation surface."
plot(log10(acc + 1), col = hcl.colors(50, "Blues", rev = TRUE),
     mar = c(2, 2, 2, 4), plg = list(title = "log10 cells"))
plot(st_geometry(st_transform(ws_poly, 3857)), add = TRUE,
     border = "orangered", lwd = 2)
plot(st_geometry(basin), add = TRUE, border = "black", lwd = 1.5, lty = 2)
plot(st_geometry(lake),  add = TRUE, col = "steelblue", border = NA)
```

## Stream networks

A stream is a cell whose accumulation exceeds a threshold. The threshold is a
choice, not a property of the landscape: lower it and ephemeral gullies appear,
raise it and only trunk rivers survive. Strahler ordering
[@strahler1957quantitative] then ranks the network, with headwaters at order one
and the order rising wherever two channels of equal rank meet:

```{r}
wbt_extract_streams(flow_accum = "acc.tif", output = "streams.tif", wd = wd,
                    threshold = 1000, zero_background = TRUE,
                    verbose_mode = FALSE)

wbt_strahler_stream_order(d8_pntr = "d8.tif", streams = "streams.tif",
                          output = "order.tif", wd = wd,
                          zero_background = TRUE, verbose_mode = FALSE)

order_rast <- rast(paste0(wd, "order.tif"))
table(values(order_rast)[values(order_rast) > 0])
```

The counts fall off geometrically, which is Horton's law of stream numbers and a
useful sanity check: a network that does not thin as order rises has usually
been built on a broken pointer raster. Retaining order three and above leaves
the primary channels, the ones a field team could actually find.

Raster streams are awkward to work with. Vectorising them gives line geometry
that can be measured, buffered and joined, and dropping short fragments first
removes the single-cell spurs that raster thinning always leaves behind:

```{r}
wbt_remove_short_streams(d8_pntr = "d8.tif", streams = "streams.tif",
                         output = "streams_long.tif", wd = wd,
                         min_length = 200, verbose_mode = FALSE)

wbt_raster_streams_to_vector(streams = "streams_long.tif", d8_pntr = "d8.tif",
                             output = "streams.shp", wd = wd,
                             verbose_mode = FALSE)

streams_v <- st_read(paste0(wd, "streams.shp"), quiet = TRUE)
st_crs(streams_v) <- 3857

data.frame(
  reaches      = nrow(streams_v),
  total_km     = round(sum(as.numeric(st_length(st_transform(streams_v, 32736)))) / 1000, 1),
  ref_reaches  = nrow(rivers),
  ref_total_km = round(sum(rivers$LENGTH_KM, na.rm = TRUE), 1)
)
```

The derived network is far denser than the HydroRIVERS reference, which is the
expected consequence of a 1,000-cell threshold against a global product built
for cartographic legibility. Neither is wrong. The comparison is only meaningful
once both are cut to the same threshold, and a project quoting stream length
without quoting its extraction threshold has quoted nothing.

### Wetness index

Flow accumulation says how much water arrives at a cell. Slope says how fast it
leaves. The topographic wetness index combines them as the log of the ratio, and
it is the standard proxy for where soil stays saturated:

The `sca` argument wants specific catchment area and the `slope` argument wants
a slope raster in degrees. Passing the DEM to the second, which is an easy slip
because both are elevation-shaped files, returns numbers that look plausible and
mean nothing:

```{r}
wbt_slope(dem = "filled.tif", output = "slope_deg.tif",
          wd = wd, verbose_mode = FALSE)

wbt_wetness_index(sca = "acc.tif", slope = "slope_deg.tif",
                  output = "twi.tif", wd = wd, verbose_mode = FALSE)

twi <- rast(paste0(wd, "twi.tif"))
global(twi, quantile, probs = c(0.05, 0.5, 0.95), na.rm = TRUE)
```

High values mark valley floors and flat convergent ground, low values mark steep
divides. For carbon work the index is a cheap covariate for soil organic carbon
and for the wetland and seasonally flooded classes that land-cover maps confuse
most often. It is derived, not observed, and it inherits every artefact of the
conditioning that produced the accumulation surface, so it belongs in a model as
a predictor rather than in a report as a measurement.

The primary channels, mapped against the reference network:

```{r}
#| fig-cap: "Strahler stream network of order three and above, with the delineated catchment and the HydroRIVERS reference channels."
major <- ifel(order_rast >= 3, order_rast, NA)

plot(hs, col = grey(40:100 / 100), legend = FALSE, mar = c(2, 2, 2, 4))
plot(st_geometry(rivers), add = TRUE, col = "grey55", lwd = 0.4)
plot(major, add = TRUE, col = hcl.colors(4, "Teal", rev = TRUE),
     plg = list(title = "Order"))
plot(st_geometry(st_transform(ws_poly, 3857)), add = TRUE,
     border = "orangered", lwd = 1.5)
plot(st_geometry(lake), add = TRUE, col = "steelblue", border = NA)
```

## Inundation mapping

Wetland accounting asks a direct question of a DEM: given a water level, what
land floods? For Chilwa the question is not academic. The lake's area is the
quantity a wetland carbon project would be crediting, and it is not stable.

The method is a threshold on elevation. Cells below the chosen level flood. Two
refinements make it work. The flooded set is restricted to the catchment
delineated above, and then to the connected depression that actually contains
the lake, found by labelling connected components. Without both, the threshold
floods every unrelated valley in the raster.

### Anchoring the datum

The level to sweep around has to come from somewhere, and the obvious choice is
wrong. Taking the minimum elevation inside the lake polygon gives this:

```{r}
lake_elev <- ifel(!is.na(lake_mask), dem, NA)
global(lake_elev, quantile, probs = c(0, 0.05, 0.5, 0.95, 1), na.rm = TRUE)
```

The minimum is seventy metres below the median, and the maximum is four hundred
metres above it. Neither extreme is the lake. The low value is an interpolation
pit, a single cell of DEM noise, and the high value is ground inside a polygon
drawn at a coarser resolution than the raster. A rating curve anchored on the
minimum sits seventy metres below the water and returns zero area at every step
a wetland project would care about.

The median is robust to both, and it is what the DEM actually recorded as the
water surface:

```{r}
surface_m <- as.numeric(global(lake_elev, quantile, probs = 0.5, na.rm = TRUE))
surface_m
```

Six hundred and twenty-three metres, which is the elevation Lake Chilwa is
conventionally given.

### Stage and area

```{r}
dem_ws <- mask(dem, ws)

flood_at <- function(level_m) {
  wet   <- ifel(dem_ws <= level_m, 1, NA)
  parts <- patches(wet, directions = 8)
  keep  <- unique(values(ifel(!is.na(lake_mask), parts, NA)))
  keep  <- keep[!is.na(keep)]
  if (!length(keep)) return(0)
  global(ifel(parts %in% keep, cell_ha, NA), "sum", na.rm = TRUE)[1, 1]
}

round(flood_at(surface_m) / 100, 1)
```

At the surface the DEM itself records, the threshold returns an area within
about one per cent of the independently mapped HydroLAKES extent. That is a
real check, and it is worth more than the single number: it says the method and
the datum are both sound before any counterfactual level is imposed on them.

The deliverable is the rating curve. Sweep the level, record the area, and you
have the relationship between stage and flooded extent that drives flood risk,
wetland carbon and reservoir planning:

```{r}
offsets  <- c(-2, -1, 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 6)
areas_ha <- vapply(surface_m + offsets, flood_at, numeric(1))

curve <- data.frame(
  stage_m  = offsets,
  level_m  = surface_m + offsets,
  area_km2 = round(areas_ha / 100, 1)
)
curve
```

```{r}
#| fig-cap: "Stage-area rating curve for the Chilwa basin. The dashed line is the HydroLAKES mapped extent."
plot(curve$stage_m, curve$area_km2, type = "b", pch = 16,
     xlab = "Stage relative to recorded surface (m)",
     ylab = "Flooded area (km2)")
abline(h = as.numeric(st_area(st_transform(lake, 32736))) / 1e6,
       col = "steelblue", lty = 2)
abline(v = 0, col = "grey70", lty = 3)
```

The curve is violently asymmetric about the recorded surface. Two metres below
it the lake is gone, a residue of two square kilometres. One metre below it,
about a seventh of its mapped area survives. Six metres above it the lake is
nearly twice its mapped size. This is not a defect of the method; it is the
lake. Chilwa's bed is almost flat, so stage and area are related by the area of
the shoreline contour, which here is enormous.

Three consequences follow, and they are the reason this method is worth a
chapter rather than a footnote.

A wetland-area claim that does not state its assumed water level is
unverifiable. The same one metre of stage uncertainty that would be immaterial
in a steep catchment moves Chilwa's area by a factor of seven.

The vertical accuracy of the DEM sets the floor on what can be claimed.
Radar-derived elevation at this resolution carries several metres of error, and
several metres here spans the entire range from dry lake to double extent. No
amount of care in the horizontal delineation compensates.

The agreement with HydroLAKES at zero stage is a consistency check, not a
validation. HydroLAKES maps a long-run maximum extent, and the DEM records the
surface on whatever dates its source scenes were acquired. That the two coincide
is reassuring about the datum and says nothing about any particular year.
Confirming a predicted extent requires a water mask from imagery acquired on a
known date, which is the subject of the activity data chapter.

::: {.verification}
#### Under wetland methodologies {.unnumbered .unlisted}

A project claiming wetland area from a DEM threshold has to defend three things,
and a verifier should ask for all three in writing.

The first is the datum and the stage. An elevation threshold is meaningless
without the vertical datum of the DEM and the water level it assumes, expressed
in that datum. A level quoted in metres above an unnamed reference is not
checkable.

The second is the sensitivity. The rating curve above is the evidence: the
project should show how much its claimed area moves for a plausible error in
stage, and a curve this steep will not support a point estimate quoted without
an interval.

The third is independent confirmation. A DEM threshold predicts inundation; it
does not observe it. Confirmation requires an optical or radar water mask from a
dated acquisition, compared against the prediction at the stage recorded on that
date. Where the two disagree the mask governs, because it is a measurement and
the threshold is a model.
:::

::: {.verification}
#### Alternative implementations {.unnumbered .unlisted}

`WhiteboxTools` is not the only route. The `flowdem` package wraps RichDEM
[@barnes2014priority], whose priority-flood algorithms take almost no parameters
and are convenient for exploratory work:

```{r}
#| eval: false
library(flowdem)

conditioned <- rast("data/chilwa_dem.tif") |>
  breach() |>
  fill(epsilon = TRUE)

dirs_fd <- dirs(conditioned, mode = "d8")
acc_fd  <- accum(dirs_fd, mode = "d8")
```

Two differences matter when choosing between them.

The parameter surface. `flowdem::breach()` takes no depth constraint, so it
carves without limit. On this DEM that produces elevation changes an order of
magnitude larger than the constrained WhiteboxTools run, across a wider extent.
Neither is right in general, but only one lets a project state what it did.

The reporting burden. `fill(epsilon = TRUE)` adds an infinitesimal gradient to
flat ground, which is the same job `flat_increment` does above. A method
statement that says "depressions were filled" without naming the algorithm, the
depth constraint and the flat increment does not describe a reproducible
procedure, and two implementations of "the same" conditioning step can differ by
hundreds of metres on ground like this.
:::

## Exercises

1. Report the relief of the Chilwa DEM, the difference between its maximum and
   minimum elevation. Derive slope and describe where the steepest ground lies
   relative to the lake.

2. Recompute the delineated catchment area from the raw cell count, using the
   one-cell-one-hectare shortcut, and compare it against the `cellSize()` figure
   in the text. By what percentage does the shortcut err, and in which
   direction?

3. Rerun the breaching step with `max_depth = 10` instead of 3. How many more
   cells change, and does the lake basin survive? Explain what a project would
   lose by conditioning too aggressively.

4. Lower the stream extraction threshold from 1,000 cells to 100. How does the
   Strahler order distribution change, and which order now corresponds to the
   channels that HydroRIVERS maps?

5. *(Applied.)* A project claims a 500 square kilometre seasonal wetland. Using
   the rating curve, state the stage that claim implies and the stage error that
   would move the area by ten per cent. Identify the single assumption that most
   affects your answer, and say what evidence you would require to close it.

